WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.566
Potential difference becomes much easier when it is read as an energy story.

2
00:00:04.566 --> 00:00:09.133
A battery transfers energy to charges and drives them around the circuit.

3
00:00:09.133 --> 00:00:13.699
Potential difference tells how much energy is transferred to each coulomb of charge.

4
00:00:13.699 --> 00:00:18.265
So voltage is not simply the total energy in the battery and it is not the current.

5
00:00:18.265 --> 00:00:22.832
It is the energy transferred per unit charge.

6
00:00:22.832 --> 00:00:27.398
That meaning is what lets later equations and circuit comparisons stay sensible.

7
00:00:27.398 --> 00:00:31.965
Keep this takeaway in view: Potential difference tells energy transferred per coulomb of charge.

8
00:00:31.965 --> 00:00:36.531
Battery role means energy transfer.

9
00:00:36.531 --> 00:00:41.097
The battery gives energy to the charges.

10
00:00:41.097 --> 00:00:45.664
Potential difference means energy per coulomb.

11
00:00:45.664 --> 00:00:50.230
Voltage tells how much energy each coulomb gains.

12
00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:57.576
A good picture for potential difference is a battery giving each coulomb a measured energy boost.

13
00:00:57.576 --> 00:01:04.921
The charges then move around the circuit and transfer that energy in components such as lamps or resistors.

14
00:01:04.921 --> 00:01:12.267
The diagram matters because it separates the moving charge from the energy being carried and transferred.

15
00:01:12.267 --> 00:01:19.613
The same coulomb can move around the loop, but the battery still tells us how much energy each coulomb gains as it passes through the supply.

16
00:01:19.613 --> 00:01:26.959
Keep this takeaway in view: Keep the voltage story attached to energy transfer for each coulomb.

17
00:01:26.959 --> 00:01:34.304
Battery action: The supply transfers energy to the charges.

18
00:01:34.304 --> 00:01:41.650
Per coulomb: Voltage belongs to each coulomb, not just to the whole circuit at once.

19
00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:46.621
Imagine identical boxes being lifted one by one to a higher shelf.

20
00:01:46.621 --> 00:01:51.592
If each box gains the same amount of gravitational energy, then the system has a fixed energy gain per box.

21
00:01:51.592 --> 00:01:56.563
That is similar to potential difference in a circuit.

22
00:01:56.563 --> 00:02:01.534
Each coulomb of charge gains a certain amount of energy from the battery.

23
00:02:01.534 --> 00:02:06.505
A higher voltage means each coulomb is lifted by a larger energy amount.

24
00:02:06.505 --> 00:02:11.475
The comparison helps because it turns the word per into something visual and consistent.

25
00:02:11.475 --> 00:02:16.446
Keep this takeaway in view: Voltage is about the energy given to each unit of charge.

26
00:02:16.446 --> 00:02:21.417
Box means coulomb.

27
00:02:21.417 --> 00:02:26.388
Each identical box stands for one unit of charge.

28
00:02:26.388 --> 00:02:31.359
Lift height means energy gain.

29
00:02:31.359 --> 00:02:36.330
How much energy each box gains mirrors the battery's energy transfer per coulomb.

30
00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:41.397
The relation is V equals E divided by Q.

31
00:02:41.397 --> 00:02:46.465
Read it as potential difference equals energy transferred divided by charge.

32
00:02:46.465 --> 00:02:51.532
That means if the same energy is shared across fewer coulombs, each coulomb gets more, so the voltage is larger.

33
00:02:51.532 --> 00:02:56.599
If the same energy is spread across more coulombs, the voltage is smaller.

34
00:02:56.599 --> 00:03:01.666
The symbols are only useful because the meaning is already stable: voltage tells the energy transferred to each coulomb.

35
00:03:01.666 --> 00:03:06.734
Keep this takeaway in view: V = E / Q is the energy-per-coulomb story written in symbols.

36
00:03:06.734 --> 00:03:11.801
Bigger E: More energy for the same charge means a larger potential difference.

37
00:03:11.801 --> 00:03:16.868
Bigger Q: The same energy spread across more charge means a smaller potential difference.

38
00:03:16.868 --> 00:03:21.935
E = VQ.

39
00:03:21.935 --> 00:03:27.003
Use energy transferred and charge moved.

40
00:03:27.003 --> 00:03:32.070
Then read the answer as joules per coulomb.

41
00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:37.568
Across a battery, potential difference tells how much energy each coulomb gains.

42
00:03:37.568 --> 00:03:43.066
Across a lamp or resistor, potential difference tells how much energy each coulomb transfers away in that component.

43
00:03:43.066 --> 00:03:48.564
So the same quantity can describe supply and transfer in different parts of the circuit.

44
00:03:48.564 --> 00:03:54.062
The key is to keep the phrase per coulomb in your mind.

45
00:03:54.062 --> 00:03:59.560
That keeps voltage meaningful whether the question is talking about the source or a particular component.

46
00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:05.058
Keep this takeaway in view: Voltage always tracks energy change per coulomb, wherever it is measured.

47
00:04:05.058 --> 00:04:10.556
Across supply means energy gained.

48
00:04:10.556 --> 00:04:16.054
Each coulomb receives energy from the battery.

49
00:04:16.054 --> 00:04:21.552
Across component means energy transferred.

50
00:04:21.552 --> 00:04:27.050
Each coulomb gives energy to the component.

51
00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:31.716
Students often say a battery has more voltage because it has more charge, or because more current is flowing.

52
00:04:31.716 --> 00:04:36.381
Those are different ideas.

53
00:04:36.381 --> 00:04:41.047
Potential difference is not the amount of charge and it is not the current.

54
00:04:41.047 --> 00:04:45.712
It is the energy transferred for each coulomb.

55
00:04:45.712 --> 00:04:50.378
That means a circuit can have a high voltage without automatically having a high current, because current depends on the circuit resistance as well.

56
00:04:50.378 --> 00:04:55.043
Keeping the meaning precise now prevents trouble in the next lesson.

57
00:04:55.043 --> 00:04:59.709
Keep this takeaway in view: The key word in the lesson is per: energy per coulomb.

58
00:04:59.709 --> 00:05:04.374
Voltage is not current.

59
00:05:04.374 --> 00:05:09.040
Voltage is not total energy on its own.

60
00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:12.755
Check yourself quickly.

61
00:05:12.755 --> 00:05:16.471
What does one volt mean?

62
00:05:16.471 --> 00:05:20.186
One joule of energy transferred per coulomb of charge.

63
00:05:20.186 --> 00:05:23.902
If a battery has a larger potential difference, what changes for each coulomb?

64
00:05:23.902 --> 00:05:27.617
Each coulomb gains more energy.

65
00:05:27.617 --> 00:05:31.332
And what formula links voltage, energy, and charge?

66
00:05:31.332 --> 00:05:35.048
V equals E divided by Q.

67
00:05:35.048 --> 00:05:38.763
These three answers are enough to make the worked example feel direct rather than mysterious.

68
00:05:38.763 --> 00:05:42.478
Keep this takeaway in view: Explain volts in words first, then calculate.

69
00:05:42.478 --> 00:05:46.194
1 V means 1 J/C.

70
00:05:46.194 --> 00:05:49.909
One volt means one joule transferred to each coulomb.

71
00:05:49.909 --> 00:05:53.625
Higher voltage means more energy each.

72
00:05:53.625 --> 00:05:57.340
Each coulomb gains a larger energy amount.

73
00:05:57.340 --> 00:06:02.977
You are ready for the worked example now.

74
00:06:02.977 --> 00:06:08.614
If 18 joules are transferred to 3 coulombs, use V equals E divided by Q because potential difference means energy transferred per coulomb.

75
00:06:08.614 --> 00:06:14.251
Eighteen divided by three gives 6 volts.

76
00:06:14.251 --> 00:06:19.888
Read that answer properly: each coulomb gains 6 joules of energy.

77
00:06:19.888 --> 00:06:25.525
That sentence is the real meaning of the 6 volt result, and it is what the lesson wants you to carry into the worked example.

78
00:06:25.525 --> 00:06:31.162
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: use the energy-per-coulomb meaning, then calculate.

79
00:06:31.162 --> 00:06:36.799
Step 1: Identify the energy transferred and the charge moved.

80
00:06:36.799 --> 00:06:42.436
Step 2: Use V = E / Q to calculate the potential difference.

81
00:06:42.436 --> 00:06:48.073
V = 18 / 3.

82
00:06:48.073 --> 00:06:53.710
Read 6 V as 6 joules of energy transferred to each coulomb.
